blocks.en:
Assume that $\Omega$ is bounded, connected, and has the $1$-extension property. Let\[V=\left\{u\in H^1(\Omega)\ \middle|\ \int_\Omega u(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=0\right\}.\](a) Show that for each $f\in L^2(\Omega)$ there is a unique $u\in V$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mean-zero-neumann-variational-problem}
\int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v=\int_\Omega fv\quad\text{for all }v\in V.
\end{equation}
(See Problem~\ref{ex:mean-zero-sobolev-poincare-inequality}.)

(b) Explain why it is appropriate to regard \eqref{eq:mean-zero-neumann-variational-problem} as a weak form of the Neumann problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mean-zero-neumann-strong-problem}
\begin{aligned}
-\Delta u&=f&&\text{in }\Omega\\
\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}&=0&&\text{on }\partial\Omega
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
if $\int_\Omega f=0$.

(c) If $\int_\Omega f\ne0$, is it still reasonable to call the solution of \eqref{eq:mean-zero-neumann-variational-problem} a solution of \eqref{eq:mean-zero-neumann-strong-problem}? Explain.

blocks.zh:
假设 $\Omega$ 有界, 连通且具有 $1$ 阶延拓性质. 令\[V=\left\{u\in H^1(\Omega)\ \middle|\ \int_\Omega u(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=0\right\}.\](a) 证明, 对每个 $f\in L^2(\Omega)$, 都存在唯一的 $u\in V$, 使得
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mean-zero-neumann-variational-problem}
\int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v=\int_\Omega fv\quad\text{for all }v\in V.
\end{equation}
(见 Problem~\ref{ex:mean-zero-sobolev-poincare-inequality}.)

(b) 说明当 $\int_\Omega f=0$ 时, 为什么适合将 \eqref{eq:mean-zero-neumann-variational-problem} 看作 Neumann 问题
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mean-zero-neumann-strong-problem}
\begin{aligned}
-\Delta u&=f&&\text{in }\Omega\\
\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}&=0&&\text{on }\partial\Omega
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
的弱形式.

(c) 若 $\int_\Omega f\ne0$, 仍将 \eqref{eq:mean-zero-neumann-variational-problem} 的解称为 \eqref{eq:mean-zero-neumann-strong-problem} 的解是否合理? 请说明.
